.TH "SKARN-ASSESS" "1" "2026-06-23" "skarn 0.35.0" "Skarn Manual"
.SH NAME
skarn\-assess \- Scan every AI session, print a redacted summary. Free, no
license
.SH SYNOPSIS
\fBskarn\fR \fBassess\fR [options]
.SH DESCRIPTION
Scan every AI session on this machine and print a friendly risk summary, with
an optional shareable redacted report and a scoped incident dossier. Free, no
config, no license. Run at a terminal it also names, once per release, a hook
in your own per\-user assistant config that has fallen behind this build, so
an event a newer template gates does not go unscanned unnoticed; it looks at
that config only, never at a repo's committed hooks and never at a
fleet\-managed one.
.PP
The zero\-config machine\-scan wedge: scan every AI coding session on the
machine and print a friendly risk summary, no flags and no license. \-o writes
a shareable redacted report (HTML or Markdown); \-\-json emits the redacted
scan for scripting. The scan is scoped by the same filters as check. The
Findings line says how many critical findings are attack chains or behaviors
with no secret value, so a reader knows why the exposures list below can hold
fewer rows than the critical count. The summary, the Markdown report and the
HTML report show one row per distinct secret value per session file, naming
the other rules that matched it after also matched; \-\-json keeps every
incident and marks the primary with also_matched, a member with grouped_under,
and stats.distinct_secret_values with the number of primaries, one per
distinct value per session file.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
\fB\-o\fR, \fB\-\-out\fR \fIfile\fR
Write a self\-contained, redacted share report to this file; .md or .markdown
renders Markdown, any other extension renders HTML. The report names a session
file by its basename and a project by its display name, and writes a home
directory inside a code\-context snippet as ~. The Markdown report ends each
exposure line with the finding's confidence and adds likely test value when
that flag is set; the HTML report shows both in the finding drawer. Both
reports open with the Act on this first section that the check text format
prints, and its line of deprioritized values; in the HTML report each row
opens its finding drawer, and selecting a tool, a project or a user hides the
section. The file is written atomically as a new owner\-only file on macOS and
Linux (on Windows it takes the permissions of its directory): a regular file
at the path, or a symlink to one, is replaced by the new file; a device or
FIFO at the path is written in place and keeps its own permissions, and so is
a symlink to one of those or to a process stream such as /dev/stdout; a
directory at the path is refused.
.TP
\fB\-\-json\fR
Emit the redacted scan as JSON (the same shape as the serve /api/scan view)
for scripting, instead of the friendly summary. Each incident carries
confidence and likely_test_value, the same values `skarn check \-\-format
json` reports as confidence and likelyTestValue. A ranked incident carries
triage_rank (1 to 5), triage_reasons (its confidence, active use, issuer class
and attack chain labels) and triage_seen (the YYYY\-MM\-DD date its recency
was ranked on), and stats carries triage_more and the three deprioritized
counts deprioritized_likely_test_value, deprioritized_low_confidence and
deprioritized_suppressed. Every other incident carries triage_rank null, an
empty triage_reasons array and an empty triage_seen. A dossier, and an
/api/scan response filtered by cli, project or user, carries every incident at
those defaults and the four counts at 0, so no section renders there. With
\-\-dossier the body is the dossier envelope that wraps this scan, not the
bare scan shape.
.TP
\fB\-\-offline\fR
Disable all network access. Assess is already egress\-free; this is the
belt\-and\-suspenders guarantee.
.TP
\fB\-\-hours\fR \fIn\fR
Scan window in hours; 0 (the default) scans every session on the machine. The
default is 0.
.TP
\fB\-\-severity\fR \fIlevel\fR
Only summarize findings at or above this severity; the default (low) scans
everything and curates the display. One of: low, medium, high, critical. The
default is low.
.TP
\fB\-\-cli\fR \fIname\fR
Restrict the scan to one assistant's sessions. If this machine has no tool by
that name, skarn exits 6 rather than scanning nothing. A custom tool declared
in ~/.skarn.json is accepted by its configured name. One of: claude, gemini,
codex, cursor, copilot, kimi, grok, grokbot, antigravity, opencode, cline,
copilot\-code.
.TP
\fB\-\-project\fR \fIpath\fR
Restrict the scan to matching projects: a comma\-separated list of names or
paths, matched case\-insensitively against a session's project display name,
its canonical root path, or a parent directory of that root. A session read
from a Compliance API export carries no project, so any \-\-project value
excludes it.
.TP
\fB\-\-session\fR \fIid\fR
Assess a single session by id.
.TP
\fB\-\-rules\fR \fIpath\fR
Load a custom TOML rules file in addition to the bundled rules. A rule pattern
whose compiled form exceeds 16 KiB is refused like any pattern that fails to
compile, so skarn exits 6 without scanning; an allowlist pattern over that
bound is skipped, so its suppression does not apply.
.TP
\fB\-\-baseline\fR \fIpath\fR
Suppress findings recorded in this accepted\-findings baseline. Without this
flag, ~/.config/skarn/baseline.json is applied automatically when it exists. A
directory of baselines requires a Pro license; assess never gates, so an
unlicensed directory degrades to a warning and an unsuppressed scan.
.TP
\fB\-\-no\-color\fR
Disable colored output.
.TP
\fB\-\-dossier\fR \fIselector\fR
Scope \-o and \-\-json to one incident dossier: one finding by its
64\-character fingerprint, or one session by id, auto\-detected by shape. A
reused credential matches every session it appears in. The dossier holds the
redacted facts Skarn has for the selection, plus an explicit list of the CRA
Article 14 reporting fields Skarn cannot supply. It is a local share artifact;
Skarn does not file reports or determine severity. Every format names a
project by its display name. The HTML and JSON dossiers name a session file by
its basename, write a home directory inside a code\-context snippet as ~, and
leave out the project root and the user and workspace ids. The Markdown
dossier prints none of those fields except the basename of a selected session
file that held a record the scan could not parse, and it states the coverage
counters of the whole scan the dossier was drawn from. The HTML dossier shows
the coverage warning of the HTML report whenever that scan was incomplete, and
its compliance export notice whenever that scan withheld export sessions.
.TP
\fB\-\-user\fR \fIname\fR
Restrict the scan to matching users: a comma\-separated list of user ids or
email addresses, where an id matches exactly and an email address matches
case\-insensitively. Only a session read from a Compliance API export carries
either, so on a machine without an export, or without the Enterprise license
that reading one needs, every \-\-user value matches nothing.
